   299	\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting comparison on the existing records (exploratory) shows evaluation discrepancies that call for further search checks, also in unflagged cells: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). Winners are selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds, and picking the better D2+H3 winner on the evaluation seeds favours it, so this is a discrepancy, not a demonstrated search failure. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
