11          What the results show and what they do not
What the two tests support together. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn,
the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled
market, with R3’s registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule’s
frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities
beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section 9, which holds the parameters and search
budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not
establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the
comparison with C∗ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do
not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At ρ = 0.2, pacing slightly raises
the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at ρ = 1, the terminal reserve that pacing adds
exhausts the allowance and causes R3’s collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.




                                                                                    17
What they do not support. The selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross ef-
ficiency of all three core rules by a similar amount while earning less than the truthful reference
against R3 and C∗ ; that is a loss relative to honest play, not necessarily a negative profit. Exploratory
mixtures expose policies omitted by the prescribed W1 search that have substantially lower empir-
ical efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; their population profitability remains unestablished,
and the favourable W1 result for R3 and C∗ is not a lower bound on efficiency. Robustness to
the searched families is not established: the doubling checks find missed improvements but cannot
bound the remaining gap, and several decisions hold only for the prescribed search procedure. The
fresh-market configurations differ from the registered ones in the last binary digit; re-evaluating the
frozen policies and references with the registered configurations leaves every Q1 and Q4 decision
unchanged, a check that does not repeat search or reference selection.

Exact but costly checks. The model’s audit already reveals delivered quality exactly, at a cost
charged to the operator’s budget. An execution proof such as FlashProve checks a different object,
that a committed computation ran, and does not remove delivery noise. It fits the model’s audit
cost only if the operator funds both proving and verification; provider-funded proving would need
separate incentive accounting. Either way, which jobs to check and how to sanction remain the
questions; if the cost of a proof fell close to that of the computation itself, the case for sampled
audits would weaken. This discussion is outside the registered tests and changes none of their
claims.

Limits. The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither
equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition 1 establishes a
profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every
studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets
come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3’s
registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities.
With honest churn the pooled advantage over C∗ is unresolved at the two lowest rates and negative
from 0.03, and the pooled advantage over B3 changes sign at the highest (Section 10); that follow-
up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator,
and leaves the comparison with C∗ unresolved at the lowest rate. The comparator was selected on
gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.


12     Conclusion
For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against
honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next
allocation, free replacement removes the ban’s continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and
without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for
B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three
of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-
history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets
under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in
sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule’s strongest family,
it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood.
At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about
0.840 against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C∗ . The


                                                   18
registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near 0.997); exploratory
mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated
gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most
of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker
replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C∗ minus
R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level t intervals. When honest providers
also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker’s
jobs it audits falls from 0.366 to 0.042 between p = 0.01 and 0.05, and its measured advantage
reverses: at p = 0.05, C∗ minus R3 is −0.173 and B3 minus R3 is −0.052 (−0.033 with the
doubled searches; the frozen value includes one failed search). Against C∗ , the one verified-history
comparator run under churn, the comparison is unresolved at p = 0.01 and 0.02, and at 0.03 C∗
minus R3 is negative on every market under both search versions; against uniform audits its pooled
advantage lasts through p = 0.03 but not to 0.05. These conclusions concern frozen searches and
references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests,
including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be
released with the paper.

Availability. The paper artifact will have its own release commit and archive hash. It identifies
the frozen evaluation code by the signed tag protocol-v2 and preserves the original tagged-code
report. It contains the code, the tuned files, all records of both tests (including incomplete, failed,
and cut cells), the market generator, the block headers, the released H4 bundle, and the devia-
tion evidence, and, separately dated, the decision-stability re-evaluation, the exploratory post hoc
analyses with the per-seed evaluation and training inputs of the lottery analysis, the prediction-
scoring correction with its regression test, the inference audit, and the paper sources. One com-
mand (docs/revision/deviation/reproduce_deviation.py) checks the artifact against the tag
and reproduces the second test’s report under the disclosed configuration substitution; the paper’s
second-test tables and figures are regenerated from the tagged report, the disclosed stability out-
put, and the exploratory post hoc and audit outputs by the scripts in docs/revision/paper-v2,
docs/revision/posthoc, and docs/revision/audit, and the first test’s records and verdict are in
the release as recorded in October 2026.


A     Further registered results
Which parts of R3 matter (Q2). Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the
fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are −0.083 for the priority phase P and −0.095 for newest-
first residual targeting G, both reductions, and 0.004 for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: 0.005).
The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G’s
conditional effects, is 0.082; G changes the gain by −0.177 without the priority phase and by only
−0.014 with it, and the priority phase changes it by only −0.001 once G is in place (averaged over
both pacing settings). R3’s own comparison, F111 minus F011 (exploratory, from the evaluation
records), is −0.003 pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the
same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (−0.015) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase’s
effect given newest-first targeting is larger (−0.017 under D2+H3 and −0.018 under X7-SC, averaged
over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting’s effect without the priority phase
(above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority
phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker
capacity, half of a round’s audits may no longer cover one provider’s jobs, and the substitution need


